{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_e45kcr6aaxl7lhgzpqnbn7u5fouv7xdbzg7u4hwh4p6dpljnaqva","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass; **5 real bugs found and fixed in the Rust**.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n| Gate | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md`: `nm -D` diff | **empty** — 20/20 symbols, 0 missing, 0 extras |\n| Phase B: every `CONFIGS.md` row | **94/94** pass across randomized inputs |\n| Binary stdout comparison | **N/A** — no `add_executable`, no `[[bin]]`, no `main.rs` (asserted by a test) |\n| Phase C: every `ERRORS.md` row | **52/52** have a passing error-path test |\n| Every feature combination | `Cargo.toml` has no `[features]`; verified in **debug *and* release** cdylib |\n\n97 tests, 0 failures, 0 warnings, from a clean rebuild.\n\n## The bugs\n\nAll five were **NaN-payload propagation** errors: SSE `xxxss dst, src` returns the quieted `dst` when it's NaN, else `src`, so `addss(a,b) ≠ addss(b,a)` when both are NaN. I derived the correct operand roles from `objdump` of the C `.so` rather than guessing:\n\n1. **`c2Dot`/`lm_dot2`** — the `addss` destination is the *second* product, not the first. Caught by the test: C returned `0x7fffffff`, Rust `0x7fc00001`.\n2. **`c2CircletoCircle`** — `addss(B.r, A.r)`, not `addss(A.r, B.r)`.\n3. **`f9`** — four of the five dot products and three of the six later multiplies had the operands transposed; replaced with one `lm_dot2` helper matching the C.\n4. **`f11`** — the source's `m = 1.0f * (...)` is **folded away by GCC**, so the C never quiets a NaN there; the Rust's literal `mulss(1.0, ...)` did. Plus four branch bodies had `addss(m, x)` instead of `addss(x, m)`.\n5. **`f13`** — `mulss(60.0, h)` and `addss(360.0, h)`: the *constant* is the destination.\n\nDetails in `translation/FIXES.md`, including non-divergences I confirmed (`f12`'s `switch` compiles to an *unsigned* `cmpl/ja`; `f3` never actually executes `INT_MIN / -1`; Rust's `%`/`.floor()` match glibc bit-for-bit).\n\n## Notes\n\n- Two things beyond the plan were needed: `cargo test` **does not rebuild a `cdylib`**, so tests were silently `dlopen`ing a stale `.so` (masking my first fix). `run_diff_tests.sh` now builds first, and the harness hard-fails if the `.so` predates `src/`.\n- Network was blocked, so `libloading` resolves from the local registry cache via `--offline`.\n- One correction went the *other* way: my `ERRORS.md` row 11 assumed `f3` was a true floored division. It isn't — the C computes `q + (v2 > 0 ? -1 : 1)`, which **adds** one when `v2 < 0` (`f3(-200,-199) == 2`). The Rust already matched; I fixed the test's invariant, not the code.\n- All float comparisons use `to_bits()`, so signed zeros and NaN payloads are part of the contract. `c_src/` is untouched apart from the cmake `build/` dir."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1694,"cache_read_input_tokens":267302,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1694,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"96cff713-b586-40b5-8e3f-d24f3966a143","uuid":"8cd28139-1071-4fb1-b21c-b355f8d663f1","timestamp":"2026-09-05T05:58:03.920Z"}
